92186045417] [17592186050984 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186068905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050984 #edb/ref 1031 "Solving Systems of Nonlinear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050984 #edb/ref 1033 410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050984 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "76fdc42b-abcd-5aad-8530-c965d0b74cc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051068 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051068 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051068 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051068 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051068 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186166790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051068 #edb/ref 1031 "Equilibrium Solutions of First-Order ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051068 #edb/ref 1033 3184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051068 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7e482952-bd90-56e1-a587-ef0d76b7d965" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221174 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221174 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221174 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c953f344-77dd-5eaa-9f57-33626a392041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221174 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](images/82/82b6d431638e3abb1c1aebf818f3fbe9f2eb92c7232a3b83c93dc6cb4e563c91.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221175 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221175 #edb/ref 1071 "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?\n\nI. Some solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually move away from it.\n\nII. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIII. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close but do not necessarily converge to the equilibrium.\n\nIV. Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and sometimes converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221175 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7405495-e774-5508-b22b-e72d82eb4346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221175 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ there exists a neighborhood $O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at some point in time $t=t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t \\to \\infty.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186221960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186221961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311602 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186221174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221412 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186221955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186221963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186374742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1039 "Understanding Definitions of Stability Types" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186221175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221413 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e4025650-a2f6-597c-9bba-8008d90aee67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186222318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1045 "Equilibrium Points and Stability for Systems of ODEs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186222315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221469 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "081b50ee-0118-5b55-ada7-29231441c411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417206 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332966" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221955 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe a **stable** equilibrium point.\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently \n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium\n{{field-2}} \nthe \n{{field-3}} \nas \n{{field-4}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28178800-4a7a-5ca7-893f-5c0dd26ea830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186221955 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start sufficiently $\\text{close to}$ the equilibrium remain close to the equilibrium $\\text{but may not converge to}$ the $\\text{equilibrium}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222315 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222315 #edb/ref 1061 588701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222315 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c2ee65c6-624f-52a8-8b0a-ed23e84593bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222315 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222316 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222316 #edb/ref 1061 588702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222316 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2be51b6-d1cc-5e3f-8242-1dc23fa98c20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222316 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222317 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186222318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222317 #edb/ref 1061 655321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222317 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2756df62-6611-50b8-b90b-649614f0beca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222317 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186222491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222318 #edb/ref 1061 588706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64234359-7fbc-5b3b-b380-92c84702a88e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186221413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222490 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that for $x'(t) = f(x),$ a point $x^\\ast$ is an *equilibrium* if $f(x^\\ast) = 0.$ Solutions starting at $x^\\ast$ remain constant for all $t.$\n\nNow, consider a system in the plane with variables $x(t)$ and $y(t){:}$\n![](images/92/92ee0672fab8119d72b73787d926a36b921e62ab18b5b6c782af901c7a1e491c.svg)\n\nA point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = (x^\\ast, y^\\ast)$ is an **equilibrium point** if $\\mathbf{f}(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast) = \\mathbf{0}.$ Both components must be zero:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{f}_{1}({x}^{∗},{y}^{∗})=0 \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}^{∗},{y}^{∗})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\mathbf{x}(t_0) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ then $\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ for all $t.$ The state does not change.\n\n**Note:** In the phase plane, $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ represents velocity. At an equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast,$ the velocity is $\\mathbf{0},$ so trajectories starting there stay there forever.\n\nFor example, let's find the equilibrium points of the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=x-y \\\\ {y}^{′}=x+y-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** Set the right-hand sides equal to zero.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=0 \\\\ x+y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Solve the system. From the first equation, $y = x.$ Substitute into the second:\n\n$$\nx + (x) - 2 = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad 2x = 2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x = 1.\n$$\n\nThus, $y = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the system has a single equilibrium point ![](images/59/5939547026b84f5a36fd2f26d7390db4c5069cdf3302217b5309699e5afe8882.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222490 #edb/ref 1065 14487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222490 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7af2084d-7c8b-577e-b656-d1040dddaabd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222490 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222491 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we have learned how to find equilibrium points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y).\n$$\n\nHowever, just knowing where equilibrium points are is not enough: we\nalso want to understand *how nearby solutions behave* when we start\nclose to an equilibrium point.\n\nRoughly speaking, there are three qualitatively different behaviors.\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **stable** if for\nany neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ there exists a neighborhood\n$O_1 \\subseteq O$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O_1$ at\nsome time $t = t_0$ remains inside $O$ for all $t > t_0.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently\nclose to the equilibrium remain close to it for all later times,\nalthough they need not converge to it.\n\n![](images/ca/ca05b3e5d076c74f4d3cf346e0598e99d363a155d41ef10b98d1914602638b76.png)\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **asymptotically\nstable** if it is stable *and* there exists a neighborhood $O$ of\n$\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$\napproaches $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions which start sufficiently\nclose to the equilibrium remain close to it *and* converge to it as\n$t \\to \\infty.$\n\nNote that asymptotic stability is a stronger condition than stability:\nit adds the requirement that nearby solutions actually reach\n$\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ in the limit.\n\n![](images/ce/cedd8e777a57901042cb46889650006a9b6e1980dc733d736790e3cc3e3ccfaa.png)\n\n- An equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ is called **unstable** if it\nis not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means there is some neighborhood $O$ of $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast$ from which trajectories can escape, even when started\narbitrarily close to $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast.$ Note that this does not\nrequire trajectories to stay outside $O$ forever; it only requires them to\nleave $O$ at some moment, even if they return later.\n\n![](images/f7/f7668e3f5ba2b8dc7b4ca3a3e503c55a54a00c2f99648895e052447274bb6d15.png)\n\n**Watch out!** These descriptions are *local*: they describe what\nhappens when the initial condition is sufficiently close to the\nequilibrium. They do not require that all trajectories in the entire\nplane behave the same way.\n\nNext, let's see some examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222491 #edb/ref 1065 15932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222491 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "398c0856-505e-5ecb-b56f-1a57b0837d91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186222491 #edb/ref 1067 "Stability, Asymptotic Stability, and Instability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305633 #edb/ref 1080 "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305633 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305633 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3326532c-6904-5726-8b28-37d12fc59053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305672 #edb/ref 1080 "Solutions approach the equilibrium only along specific trajectories." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305672 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305672 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "36132c23-5815-5cc4-8bec-9773d06472ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305980 #edb/ref 1080 "Solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305980 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305980 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "55a2abaf-34b3-5657-a1de-9ab5577e137e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306735 #edb/ref 1080 "Solutions converge to the equilibrium only if the system is linear." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306735 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306735 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a80bb591-6d0d-5b6e-a88c-9518ccdc6976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306980 #edb/ref 1080 "Solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306980 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186306980 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c452c72e-96d9-537f-b200-3958ad51d06c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307886 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307886 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307886 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307886 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "737a5434-eab8-53c1-9363-93769be84c1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308350 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308350 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308350 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308350 #edb/ref 1071 "Which statement correctly describes an **asymptotically stable** equilibrium point?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308350 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ea1f515-d00c-5fdc-9aaa-b3d0c6e88d7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308350 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSolutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218