47-5934-95a7-bf41d6dda92d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310011 #edb/ref 1080 "Unstable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310011 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310011 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9c2d2800-4e40-5903-b0a3-759d1f04991b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310532 #edb/ref 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nand \n$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310532 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310532 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de6af197-5a0f-57ed-8031-77e6206dc4fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310604 #edb/ref 1080 "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310604 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310604 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e94c9d30-fcb3-5791-9073-e5c41bd8b344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186308925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310963 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310963 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310963 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310963 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186310532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310963 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3edad7c4-0b87-5946-b745-744e4167a9d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186308952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311271 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311271 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311271 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311271 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186308952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311271 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a55ca2b0-a8ad-56c1-9d6a-fd59632a1570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311600 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311600 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311600 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311600 #edb/ref 1071 "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to remain in a bounded region around the equilibrium, sometimes oscillating or circling, but they do not converge to the equilibrium as time increases. How should the equilibrium be classified?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311600 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42763379-2213-512f-b7ee-d0d9b4e1db06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311600 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Stable but not asymptotically stable.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311616 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311616 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335647" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311616 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311616 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311616 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db5a2e69-356d-5e6a-bf94-b1893ca52642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311616 #edb/ref 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y-4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2y-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2(-x-2)-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\text{ }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-2=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=-(-2)-2=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374742 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335461" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374742 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374742 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the correct options in the statement below that describe a **stable** equilibrium point.\n\nThe solutions that start \n{{field-1}}\nthe equilibrium\n{{field-2}}\nit for all future time, and\n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374742 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e3b1a71-b3cb-54a2-a8d7-2da738fb9765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374742 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nThe solutions that start $\\text{sufficiently close to}$ the equilibrium $\\text{remain close to}$ it for all future time, and $\\text{may not converge to the equilibrium as }t\\to \\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374743 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374743 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374743 #edb/ref 1077 "fi